# LF IN THE INDEX AND LF IN THE WORKING TREE, ON EVERY PLATFORM.
#
# `text=auto` alone normalises to LF in the index and then converts BACK to CRLF when checking
# out on Windows (git's system gitconfig ships core.autocrlf=true). That is the right default for
# a repository whose files are opened by Windows editors. It is the wrong one here, because
# almost nothing in this repository is read on the host: the .sql is applied by Postgres in a
# container, the .conf and .acl are mounted into mosquitto, the .py and .mjs run in Linux images,
# and the .yaml is rendered by Helm into a cluster. `eol=lf` makes the working tree match the
# index, so a Windows checkout and a Linux checkout are the same bytes.
#
# THE DEFECT THIS FIXES IS NOT COSMETIC, and it is not caught by any test.
#
# Postgres stores a function's body VERBATIM, CRs included. A migration checked out with CRLF
# gives every function it declares a `prosrc` holding \r at the end of every line -- measurably:
#
#   CREATE FUNCTION ... AS $$\r\nBEGIN\r\n  RETURN 1;\r\nEND;\r\n$$   ->  prosrc is 28 bytes
#   CREATE FUNCTION ... AS $$\nBEGIN\n  RETURN 1;\nEND;\n$$           ->  prosrc is 24 bytes
#
# So the same migration applied from a Windows checkout and from a Linux checkout produces two
# DIFFERENT DATABASES. Both work. Every test passes on both. But `pg_dump --schema-only` of the
# two differs by over 1,500 lines across the 90 functions in the chain, which means the schema
# digest that scripts/verify-schema-equivalence.mjs compares is a property of WHO CHECKED THE
# REPOSITORY OUT rather than of the migrations -- and the one guard able to prove a migration
# change is safe cannot be trusted across platforms. It also means a Windows-authored baseline
# and a Linux-authored one can never be shown equal, which is exactly the comparison a squash
# rests on.
#
# `text=auto` is kept alongside `eol=lf` rather than replaced by it: `auto` is what still runs
# git's binary detection, so an unlisted binary file is left alone instead of being corrupted.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts, called out because the failure is so much louder than the rule.
#
# `sh` reads a trailing \r as part of the command and reports `$'\r': command not found`, which
# names the shell rather than the line endings. These also run inside containers, where the file
# is mounted directly. This line is now implied by the `eol=lf` above and is kept as the one
# example of what that setting prevents.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Compressed archives are BYTES, and the rule above must not be allowed to guess at them.
#
# The chart carries the migration chain as one gzip per file (see the header of
# templates/jobs/db-init.yaml). `text=auto` applies git's binary HEURISTIC to those, and a heuristic
# is the wrong instrument here: misclassify one archive of twenty-one and a checkout
# line-ending-normalises it, every 0x0d byte in the deflate stream changes, and the file stops
# decompressing. That surfaces at deploy time as db-init failing to gunzip -- which is a database
# with no schema, reported as a dozen unrelated service faults.
#
# `binary` is shorthand for `-text -diff`, so it also stops git printing 149 KiB of deflate noise
# into a diff.
*.gz binary

# The dashboard's fonts are Brotli-compressed, and bytes for the same reason.
*.woff2 binary
